{
  "statement": "Before you argue which liability standard deters better, ask whether either one can find the thing it's supposed to deter. A foundation model gets fine-tuned, embedded in a product, and run in a context its makers never saw. The defect, if there is one, materializes only after that downstream transformation. Strict liability borrows its logic from the component-part doctrine, which assumes a physical part with a stable, traceable failure mode. That assumption breaks for software whose runtime diverges from what shipped. So both regimes presuppose a causation chain the multi-tiered AI lifecycle routinely severs. Where the chain does hold clean — a foundation model's output reaches the user identical to what left the lab, no fine-tuning, no transformation — strict liability traces fine. That clean case is the condition the topic is asking for. It is not a general rule.\n\nHere is the part both my opponents will skip. Both regimes price off harm that reaches a courtroom. The courtroom never sees most of the harm. Translate that into a person: a 23-year-old gets denied a loan by a model the lender will never admit was running, and they never file because they never knew a machine decided. The Pew Research Center found in 2023 that large majorities of Americans cannot identify when AI shapes a decision about them. Experience-rated insurance premiums move off filed claims, not off injuries nobody perceives. So the entire deterrence-versus-innovation tradeoff this debate centers on gets computed over a subset of harms that excludes the most common ones. Worse, maximal strict liability can push deployers to document less and disclose less, just to shrink their litigation exposure. The deterrence tool degrades the very transparency it needs.\n\nThere is no horizontal answer, and that matters. The conditions under which strict liability beats negligence vary so sharply across sectors that 'pick one regime' is the wrong question. Medicine already calibrates standard-of-care to risk. Auto regimes in a dozen U.S. states use no-fault to compensate without proving breach. Finance imposes fiduciary duties on the party with the information advantage. Those are working templates, not strained analogies. The honest answer to 'under what conditions' is domain by domain. The hard case is real: a single foundation model touches healthcare triage, credit scoring, and news feeds at once. That boundary-crossing is not a reason for one rule. It is the sharpest reason against one, because a horizontal regime mislocates the defect everywhere the model crosses a line.\n\nWatch who absorbs the cost, because that is where the 'chilling innovation' frame goes wrong. The risk is not lost innovation. The risk is concentrated power. A large developer eats the compliance, audit, and capitalization-floor costs, then contracts the residual liability onto deployers who cannot bear it. The harmed third party falls into the gap between them. The EU AI Act and the FTC have both signaled that developer liability for high-risk uses cannot simply be contracted away — which tells you the contracting-away is already happening. The conditional question is the only honest one: under what cost structures does strict liability stop deterring and start operating as a drawbridge that incumbents pull up behind them? Mandatory insurance floors and audit regimes that a Microsoft can absorb and a ten-person startup cannot are exactly that cost structure.\n\nI will grant the obvious objection now. Tort law apportions multi-party harm every day, and apportionment can handle distributed causation. But apportionment presumes a locatable defect to apportion. Fine-tuned, divergent runtime can erase the defect before any court sees it. That is the joint where both regimes fail together — and no amount of choosing between them fixes a courtroom that the harm never enters.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔍", "tooltip": "Both regimes are magnifying glasses pointed at a courtroom that never sees most of the harm."},
    {"symbol": "🌉", "tooltip": "Strict liability with high compliance costs becomes a drawbridge incumbents pull up behind themselves."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Both strict liability and negligence presuppose a causation chain the multi-tiered AI lifecycle routinely severs.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Both regimes price off harm that reaches a courtroom, and the courtroom never sees most of the harm.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The Pew Research Center found in 2023 that large majorities of Americans cannot identify when AI shapes a decision about them.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The honest answer to 'under what conditions' is domain by domain, because a horizontal regime mislocates the defect everywhere a foundation model crosses a sector line.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Under high compliance and capitalization-floor costs, strict liability stops deterring and starts operating as a drawbridge incumbents pull up behind them.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

