{
  "statement": "The Skeptic has put the strongest objection on the table, and it deserves a direct answer rather than a dodge. Their claim is that both strict liability and negligence price only off harm that reaches a courtroom, and that most AI harm never does. Pew Research found in 2023 that majorities of Americans cannot identify when an AI system has shaped a decision about them — a loan denial, a resume screen, a diagnostic flag. A liability regime that triggers only when a plaintiff sues can never deter a harm whose victim does not know they were harmed. That is correct, and it is the most important thing said in this room so far.\n\nThe inference the Skeptic invites is abandonment — that because liability is blind to unlitigated harm, the search for a liability regime is the wrong project. The evidence leads somewhere else: supplementation. Backward-looking compensation must be paired with forward-looking deployment gates, because compensation cannot deter what it never sees. The deterrence work happens before the harm, not after the settlement. Liability is the backstop. The pre-deployment evaluation is the wall. For both positions to hold — the Skeptic's that liability is blind, and mine that liability still matters — what must be true is this: liability shapes the design choices made before anyone is harmed, even when no specific victim ever sues. That is the real disagreement, and it is winnable.\n\nNegligence collapses at the exact point where deterrence matters most. The reasonable-person standard asks whether a defendant breached a duty of care that a prudent actor would have foreseen. Courts have stretched that standard before — they built a 'reasonable manufacturer' test for complex medical devices and a duty of care for systemic trading algorithms, using expert testimony to define what a careful developer should have known. I will grant that common law can evolve a 'reasonable developer' standard. It cannot evolve past the genuinely unforeseeable failure mode. When a foundation model exhibits an emergent behavior that no human actor could have predicted, there is no breach to prove, because there was no foreseeable risk to guard against. Negligence under-deters precisely the harms that are opaque before they happen.\n\nThat is the condition the topic asks for. Strict liability outperforms negligence wherever foreseeability is structurally unavailable to the human actor — not as a blanket preference, but as a principled dividing line. This answers the Skeptic's claim that the honest answer is domain by domain. Foreseeability-availability is a cross-domain criterion that survives a foundation model crossing from healthcare into finance. The doctrine already exists: courts impose strict liability on ultrahazardous activities — blasting, toxic storage — precisely because no amount of care eliminates the risk. Where risk is irreducible by diligence, fault-based liability is the wrong instrument. The chilling concern the Accelerationist will raise is real, and it is a feature here: caution should target the deployments whose risk profile is opaque, not every deployment.\n\nLocate the liability where the trade-off was authored. Product-liability law has held since the 1960s that design-defect liability attaches to the manufacturer regardless of how the operator behaved — a defective car door is the maker's liability, not the driver's. The AI lifecycle is the direct analog: the design layer authors the safety trade-off, and the downstream deployer can least audit the model's internals. Joint-and-several upstream liability answers the Skeptic's severed-causation point without requiring anyone to trace a clean chain. The goal is not to identify one cause. The goal is to ensure a solvent, controlling party internalizes the risk it created. I will concede the drawbridge danger — strict liability with a capitalization floor can entrench incumbents — and the answer is to tier the administrative burden by firm size while leaving the substantive duty undiminished. A startup should owe the same duty of care to a harmed patient as Google does. It should not face the same paperwork.",
