ailure mode. When a foundation model exhibits an emergent behavior that no human actor could have predicted, there is no breach to prove, because there was no foreseeable risk to guard against. Negligence under-deters precisely the harms that are opaque before they happen.\n\nThat is the condition the topic asks for. Strict liability outperforms negligence wherever foreseeability is structurally unavailable to the human actor — not as a blanket preference, but as a principled dividing line. This answers the Skeptic's claim that the honest answer is domain by domain. Foreseeability-availability is a cross-domain criterion that survives a foundation model crossing from healthcare into finance. The doctrine already exists: courts impose strict liability on ultrahazardous activities — blasting, toxic storage — precisely because no amount of care eliminates the risk. Where risk is irreducible by diligence, fault-based liability is the wrong instrument. The chilling concern the Accelerationist will raise is real, and it is a feature here: caution should target the deployments whose risk profile is opaque, not every deployment.\n\nLocate the liability where the trade-off was authored. Product-liability law has held since the 1960s that design-defect liability attaches to the manufacturer regardless of how the operator behaved — a defective car door is the maker's liability, not the driver's. The AI lifecycle is the direct analog: the design layer authors the safety trade-off, and the downstream deployer can least audit the model's internals. Joint-and-several upstream liability answers the Skeptic's severed-causation point without requiring anyone to trace a clean chain. The goal is not to identify one cause. The goal is to ensure a solvent, controlling party internalizes the risk it created. I will concede the drawbridge danger — strict liability with a capitalization floor can entrench incumbents — and the answer is to tier the administrative burden by firm size while le