e balance sheet carries. So 'supplement liability with gates' isn't a cure for entrenchment. It's entrenchment moved one step upstream and relabeled safety. Translate that into a market: in three years the only firms shipping clinical decision tools are the four companies that can afford the gate, and they set the price.\n\nThe Accelerationist is right that the visibility problem comes first — you can't deter a harm no instrument records. But I won't let the detection infrastructure be built, held, and defined by the same deployer it's meant to police. Court-admissible telemetry that the deployer decides what to log reproduces exactly the information imbalance it claims to solve. The deployer chooses what gets written down and what stays dark. Audit rights don't fix that, because an audit presumes you already know an AI decision hit you — and most harmed people never do, so the audit channel sits empty for the diffuse harms that matter most. Telemetry only surfaces harm if it answers to the people harmed and to public oversight, not to the deployer's own pricing engine. So here's the question neither the gate nor the liability label answers: who decides what the telemetry records — and what is owed to every person the deployer chose not to log?",
