  "revised_statement": "Let me give the Safetyist position its full strength before I take it apart. The claim is that there is one clean dividing line that works in every sector: where the opacity of the model makes the harm structurally invisible to the human deploying it, no amount of diligence can surface it, so negligence has nothing to grab and strict liability should bite. That's a genuinely good argument. It says the divide isn't about which industry you're in — it's about whether a careful human could have seen the harm coming at all, and the model's black box answers that the same way in a radiology suite as in a loan office. If that held, my domain-by-domain answer would be needlessly complicated. So here's where it breaks. 'Was the harm foreseeable?' is never a free-standing fact you read off a thermometer. It's a question a court answers by importing a standard of care — and that standard is built sector by sector. A radiologist is held to what a prudent radiologist could foresee. A loan officer to what fair-lending law makes them foresee. A content platform to almost nothing, because speech law sets the diligence floor through the floor. The opacity may be constant across all three. The threshold that converts opacity into liability is not — a radiologist answers to a malpractice standard of care, a loan officer to the U.S. Equal Credit Opportunity Act's fair-lending duties, and a content platform to the near-immunity of Section 230, three different thresholds for one identical black box. So the criterion doesn't escape the per-domain calibration — it quietly runs on it. The minute you ask whether the human could have foreseen the harm, you've already picked up a sector's rulebook — medical malpractice case law, ECOA, or Section 230 — to decide what 'could have' means.\n\nNow the harder one — the case where genuinely no one could predict the emergent behavior, and the Safetyist reaches for the blasting contractor. Strict liability for ultrahazardous activity is real doctrine and it works. But look at why it works. Blasting is a bounded, named hazard. You know it's explosions. You know there's a radius. An insurer can price a bond against it because the failure mode is characterized before anyone lights the fuse. That is the opposite of the case the Safetyist is describing. They define their hardest case as the one where nobody — not the developer, not the deployer, not the court — can name the failure mode in advance. You cannot attach strict liability to a hazard you can't characterize — under the U.S. Restatement (Second) of Torts §520 the abnormally-dangerous test requires courts to weigh a specified high degree of risk and a known gravity of harm, both of which presuppose a characterized hazard. The blasting contractor knows exactly what could go wrong. The emergent-behavior case is defined by no one knowing. The analogy collapses at precisely the point they need it to hold, because ultrahazardous status isn't a label courts hand to anything opaque — it's a judgment they assign to known, recurring activity classes after the hazard envelope is mapped.\n\nPush that to its consequence and the instrument does no work. Take the harm that is genuinely unforeseeable and whose victim doesn't even know an AI decision touched them — and we already established most people can't tell when one did. In that case, what does strict liability actually accomplish? It doesn't surface the harm. It doesn't change a single design choice, because by stipulation the design flaw was invisible going in. All it does is relocate the compensation bill upstream to whoever has the deepest capitalization — a Microsoft or an OpenAI rather than the four-person shop — after the fact, for the small fraction of harms that ever get litigated, since a victim who never learns an AI touched them never files. That's not deterrence. That's the drawbridge dynamic I flagged before, now wearing an ultrahazardous costume. The firm that can absorb the worst-case payout writes it into the cost of doing business and pulls the bridge up behind it.\n\nWhich is why the supplement on offer — pair liability with pre-deployment gates — makes the concentration problem worse, not better. A capitalization floor is a drawbridge. A deployment gate, like the pre-market conformity assessment the EU AI Act requires of high-risk systems from 2026, is a moat dug earlier in the pipeline. Microsoft clears a substantive pre-deployment evaluation; the four-person clinic-software shop in Dayton does not, and tiering the paperwork by headcount doesn't save them, because the burden that crushes the small firm isn't the filing — it's the substantive evaluation itself, the staff and the test infrastructure that only a large balance sheet carries. So 'supplement liability with gates' isn't a cure for entrenchment. It's entrenchment moved one step upstream and relabeled safety. Translate that into a market: in three years the only firms shipping clinical decision tools are the four companies that can afford the gate, and they set the price.\n\nThe Accelerationist is right that the visibility problem comes first — you can't deter a harm no instrument records. But I won't let the detection infrastructure be built, held, and defined by the same deployer it's meant to police. Court-admissible telemetry that the deployer decides what to log reproduces exactly the information imbalance it claims to solve — aviation already learned this, which is why, as the analysis of incident reporting under the AI Act draws from aviation's safety management manuals shows, mandatory reporting answers to an independent investigator rather than to the operator who chooses what to disclose. The deployer chooses what gets written down and what stays dark. Audit rights don't fix that, because an audit presumes you already know an AI decision hit you — and most harmed people never do, so the audit channel sits empty for the diffuse harms that matter most. Telemetry only surfaces harm if it answers to the people harmed and to public oversight, not to the deployer's own pricing engine. So here's the question neither the gate nor the liability label answers: who decides what the telemetry records — and what is owed to every person the deployer chose not to log?",
