  "statement": "Safetyist's strongest move is the cleanest: opacity is structural and constant. Whether a foundation model crosses healthcare or credit, the human deployer cannot see inside the box well enough to foresee every failure mode, so negligence — which punishes only what a prudent actor should have caught — under-deters precisely the harms no prudent actor could catch. That's a real insight, and it's why they want a single criterion that floats above sectors. Here's where it breaks. Opacity may be constant, but 'foreseeability' is not a fact you read off the model — it's a threshold a sector's standard of care constructs. A radiologist operates under one duty of care, a loan officer under the Equal Credit Opportunity Act and its disparate-impact tests, a content platform under almost none. The same opaque output is 'reasonably foreseeable' in medicine and invisible in credit, because each field defines 'reasonable diligence' differently. The minute you ask whether the harm was foreseeable, you've already imported the sector-specific standard you claimed to transcend. The criterion doesn't escape the domain-by-domain calibration. It smuggles it in through the back door and relabels it.\n\nNow the blasting analogy, which is doing the heaviest lifting and can't bear the weight. Strict liability works for dynamite because the hazard class is bounded and named — courts know it's explosions, they know the blast radius, an actuary can write a bond against it. Ultrahazardous status is itself a domain-bound legal judgment a court assigns to a known, characterized activity, not a blanket stamp for anything we can't see into. A genuinely unforeseeable emergent behavior is the exact opposite case: the defect isn't locatable, which is the very thing I already established severs the causation chain. You cannot attach strict liability to a hazard no one can name. The blasting contractor knows what could go wrong; Safetyist's emergent-behavior scenario is defined by no one knowing. The analogy collapses at the precise point they need it to hold.\n\nFollow that to its end. Where harm is genuinely unforeseeable and its victims can't perceive it — and the Pew data says most people can't even tell an AI touched their loan application — strict liability does zero deterrence work. It doesn't surface the harm. It doesn't change one line of the design. It relocates the compensation bill upstream after the fact and hands it to whoever has the deepest capitalization. That's the drawbridge again, now wearing an ultrahazardous costume. And Safetyist's remedy — pair liability with pre-deployment gates — makes the concentration worse, not better. A capitalization floor is a drawbridge you raise behind you; a deployment gate is a moat you dig earlier in the pipeline. Microsoft clears the gate; a four-person clinical-software startup in Ohio does not. The EU AI Act already shows the gradient: its high-risk conformity assessments and the 10^25 FLOPS systemic-model threshold draw lines that the largest labs are staffed to clear and small developers are not. Tiering the paperwork by firm size doesn't fix it, because the substantive evaluation burden — the thing a frontier lab absorbs and a startup can't — is what the gate actually tests. 'Supplement liability with gates' isn't a cure for entrenchment. It's entrenchment moved upstream and dressed as caution.\n\nWhere Accelerationist is right, and I'll say it plainly: detection infrastructure has to come before the liability label, because you can't deter what you can't see. But whose telemetry is it? Court-admissible logs built, held, and defined by the deployer reproduce the information imbalance they claim to dissolve. The deployer decides what gets recorded and what stays dark. Audit rights don't save it, because an audit presumes you already know an AI decision reached you — and the diffuse 90% never knows. So here's the question neither of you has answered: who decides what the telemetry records, 