  "statement": "The strongest version of Skeptic's position is this: there is no view from nowhere on foreseeability. A radiologist owes a duty calibrated by medical malpractice law, a lender owes one calibrated by the Equal Credit Opportunity Act's disparate-impact test, and a platform owes almost nothing under Section 230. If 'foreseeability-availability' means 'whatever a reasonable actor in this sector should have caught,' then it is sector-set definitionally, and my cross-domain line is sector calibration wearing a trench coat. And the blasting analogy is worse off still — strict liability for dynamite attaches because an actuary can name the hazard, bond the blast radius, and price it. An emergent behavior nobody anticipated has no named class to attach to. That is a real argument, and on the blasting analogy it wins.\n\nSo I drop the blasting analogy. Strict liability as a hazard-class label does require a named, bounded, characterizable hazard, and a genuinely unforeseeable failure mode is unnamed by construction — you cannot bond a blast radius for an explosion no actuary has ever seen. The structural payoff of that collapse is where the disagreement actually moves: it tells you the trigger does not belong on the hazard label at all. It belongs at the deployment gate. A gate does not ask 'what specific failure will this system produce' — that question is unanswerable for emergent behavior and Skeptic is right that it is unanswerable. A gate asks a different question: 'has the deployer demonstrated detection capacity for the class of emergent failure this system can produce, before release?' Aviation already runs on exactly this distinction. The safety management framework classifies a 'serious incident' by the probability of an accident and whether one was avoided only by chance — it does not require naming the specific failure in advance, it requires demonstrated capacity to catch the class. You gate on detection capacity, not on a pre-named hazard.\n\nHere is where I split the difference Skeptic collapses. The legal threshold — what each sector calls reasonable diligence — is sector-set. I grant that without reservation; the radiologist and the credit officer face different bars and always will. But the threshold sits on top of a prior, sector-independent fact: was the signal there to be inspected at all? Could any standard of care, however calibrated, have surfaced this failure mode by examining the system before release? That antecedent question is an engineering property of the system, not a legal construct. A model whose failure was reducible by available interpretability tooling sits in one structural category; a model whose failure was irreducible by any ex-ante inspection sits in another — and that boundary does not move when you cross from medicine into credit. Skeptic's attack conflates 'what should a reasonable lender have caught' (sector-set) with 'was there anything for any inspector to catch' (system-set). I am anchoring to the second, and deliberately not to legal compliance — because if I anchored safety to what statutes currently codify, I would be installing a ceiling where I need a floor, and the laws lag the systems by years.\n\nThe honest objection that remains: Skeptic will say 'reducible by any diligence at all' is still sector-relative, because the diligence available in radiology differs from the diligence available in lending. Fair — and that is precisely why the substantive evaluation burden cannot sit on the firm. The Skeptic's earlier point that a capitalization floor is a drawbridge raised behind incumbents is correct, and I will not pretend tiering paperwork answers it; the moat is the cost of substantive evaluation, not documentation. So socialize that cost. The FDA does not require each drug company to invent clinical-trial science from scratch — it maintains the evaluative apparatus as public infrastructure. Publicly funded interpretability and evaluation commons (the kind of work funded research into model internals is meant to seed) puts the same detection harness in reach of a five-person startup and a frontier lab alike. That removes substantive evaluation from the firm-size drawbridge entirely. The burden becomes a shared public good, not a barrier you staff your way over.\n\nThat leaves the question nobody has answered: who decides what the telemetry records, and what is owed to the harms a deployer quietly chose not to log? Deployer-built, deployer-held logs reproduce the exact information imbalance they claim to dissolve — a record the harmed party never controls is a record that never has to admit they were harmed. The answer is not to trust the party with the information advantage to write its own ledger. It is an externally mandated, standardized disclosure schema — model cards, mandated incident-reporting protocols, negative-event logging defined by the governance body and audited against it, not self-reported. I will name my own trap before Skeptic does: a mandated schema can become compliance theater, a template filled in without substantive disclosure, and worse, you cannot mandate logging a failure mode you did not anticipate at schema-design time — the foreseeability problem re-enters at the schema layer. That is real. But the aviation incident framework shows the way through: you do not enumerate every failure in advance, you mandate reporting of any event where an accident was avoided only by chance. The schema logs near-misses by structural category, not by pre-named mode. The deployer who controls the ledger gets to decide which injuries were ever written down. A governance body that controls the schema does not.",
