  "statement": "The Skeptic raises a valid point: court-admissible telemetry held by the deployer reproduces the exact information imbalance it claims to dissolve, and audit rights are worthless to the people who never learn an AI shaped a decision about them. That's correct, and it's why my trigger was never built on deployer-held logs. A record the harmed party can't see is a record the deployer never has to admit exists — so the schema has to be externally mandated, owned by an agency, and disclosed on a fixed structural category, not handed to the firm that caused the harm.\n\nWhich means the Safetyist and I are closer than this debate has let on. We both want a standardized disclosure schema defined outside the deployer — model cards, incident and near-miss reporting by category, telemetry the firm cannot quietly own. The fight isn't about custody anymore. The fight is one question: what fires the obligation? My answer is the observable legal line — credit, employment, medical care. The Safetyist's answer is a deployment gate that fires when the deployer can demonstrate detection capacity for a class of emergent failure. So let's actually run those two triggers against each other instead of circling them.\n\nHere's where the detection-capacity gate breaks. The Safetyist built it on a 'system-level fact' — was there any signal there for any inspector to catch — claimed to sit underneath sector-set diligence and to be independent of it. But the Skeptic already showed that foreseeability is not a fact you read off the model; it's a threshold each sector's standard of care constructs. Apply the same blade to reducibility: the diligence available to a radiologist inspecting a diagnostic model is not the diligence available to a loan officer inspecting a credit screen. If 'was there a signal to inspect' depends on what inspection that field can actually mount, then the supposedly system-independent fact is sector-relative all the way down — and the gate collapses back into the domain-by-domain calibration it claimed to escape. The Safetyist flagged this vulnerability itself. I'm telling you it's fatal: a trigger that pretends to a vantage no one occupies is a trigger lawyers litigate for a decade. The legal line is the only boundary every party — developer, deployer, insurer, victim — can see before deployment, written into statute, not reconstructed by dueling experts after someone is already hurt.\n\nNow the moderator's two questions, which I owe straight answers on. Who builds the telemetry, and when? Not a new agency inventing interpretability science that does not yet exist. The Equal Credit Opportunity Act already gives a regulator jurisdiction over credit decisions; the EEOC already polices employment screens; the FDA already clears diagnostic devices. Each can extend a court-admissible logging mandate inside rulemaking it already runs — near-term, no new science required, because the obligation attaches to a decision class the law already names. The detection-capacity gate has no such home. It waits on interpretability research, and the Safetyist conceded the deepest part of its own case: even with a publicly funded evaluation commons, demonstrating detection capacity for emergent failure may still take frontier-scale expertise. That makes the gate the deepest drawbridge in the room — whoever defines the shared harness sets the rules, and the only firms staffed to clear the harness are the ones large enough to help write it. A public commons doesn't remove that moat. It launders it. So the real question for the Safetyist isn't whether the schema is external — we agree it is — it's why you'd anchor the trigger to a capability only your biggest competitors can prove they have.",
