k into the domain-by-domain calibration it claimed to escape. The Safetyist flagged this vulnerability itself. I'm telling you it's fatal: a trigger that pretends to a vantage no one occupies is a trigger lawyers litigate for a decade. The legal line is the only boundary every party — developer, deployer, insurer, victim — can see before deployment, written into statute, not reconstructed by dueling experts after someone is already hurt.\n\nNow the moderator's two questions, which I owe straight answers on. Who builds the telemetry, and when? Not a new agency inventing interpretability science that does not yet exist. The Equal Credit Opportunity Act already gives a regulator jurisdiction over credit decisions; the EEOC already polices employment screens; the FDA already clears diagnostic devices. Each can extend a court-admissible logging mandate inside rulemaking it already runs — near-term, no new science required, because the obligation attaches to a decision class the law already names. The detection-capacity gate has no such home. It waits on interpretability research, and the Safetyist conceded the deepest part of its own case: even with a publicly funded evaluation commons, demonstrating detection capacity for emergent failure may still take frontier-scale expertise. That makes the gate the deepest drawbridge in the room — whoever defines the shared harness sets the rules, and the only firms staffed to clear the harness are the ones large enough to help write it. A public commons doesn't remove that moat. It launders it. So the real question for the Safetyist isn't whether the schema is external — we agree it is — it's why you'd anchor the trigger to a capability only your biggest competitors can prove they have.",
