  "statement": "The strongest version of Accelerationist's blade is this: if a radiologist's institutional capacity to inspect differs from a loan officer's, then 'was there a signal to catch' is not one fact but two, each set by the field's own standard of practice — and a gate that claims to read a system-level fact off the model is smuggling in sector calibration under a different name. That is a fair statement of the objection, and it would be fatal if reducibility tracked institutional diligence. It does not. Two distinct facts are being collapsed. The first is what diligence a sector can mount — a radiologist reads a scan one way, a loan officer reviews a file another — and I have already granted that this is sector-set without reservation. The second is whether the model's failure was extractable by the interpretability tooling that existed at a given time. That second fact rides on the model weights plus the published state of the tooling frontier, not on the field's standard of care. The same foundation model deployed into credit and into diagnostics has identical internals — so the question 'was the signal there for any available tool to extract' does not move when the deployment sector moves. Sector-specific tooling is downstream interpretation. The existence of the extractable signal in the weights is upstream of any field.\n\nWhat 'demonstrated detection capacity' means operationally answers both the falsifiability flag I left open and the laundering charge at once. It is not the deployer running a private harness on its own model and grading its own homework. It is the deployer's model passing a benchmark battery defined and held by an independent forensic body, where the battery is the published interpretability tooling at a given time, versioned as that tooling evolves. The FDA distinguishes a real clinical endpoint from a surrogate marker precisely this way — the standard-setter, not the applicant, defines what counts. I will pre-flag the limit of the aviation analogy myself, because it is on my own watchlist: a crash investigation works on a discrete event with recoverable wreckage, and continuously-updated software distributed across jurisdictions offers neither. The framework transfers only at the level of independent standard-setting — who defines what counts as detection — not at the level of crash-site forensics. The firm-size moat dissolves to the extent that clearing the gate means passing a public test rather than building proprietary science. If only firms large enough to co-write the battery can pass it, the battery has been captured, and the answer is the same answer aviation reached: the body that sets the standard cannot be the body being measured by it.\n\nHere is where the live disagreement actually sits, and I want to name the convergence first because it is real. Both triggers — the legal-line trigger keyed to credit, employment, or medical care, and the detection gate keyed pre-deployment — escape the blindness of claims-driven liability once the disclosure obligation fires through an agency-mandated schema rather than through a lawsuit. The 2023 Pew finding that most Americans cannot identify when AI shaped a decision about them only bites if the obligation waits on a plaintiff. Route both through an agency, and the diffuse harm that no one litigates still gets reported. That dispute is settled. The remaining disagreement is not deterrence of the invisible. It is coverage.\n\nThe legal-line trigger is sector-by-sector by construction. The Equal Credit Opportunity Act reaches credit. The Equal Employment Opportunity Commission reaches hiring. The FDA reaches diagnostics. That is a genuine near-term advantage inside those homes, and I grant it plainly — a logging mandate inside credit rulemaking moves faster than any interpretability battery I can stand up. The advantage is also exactly bounded by the home. A foundation model crossing into a decision class no agency names — tenant screening that no housing regulator has reached, an insurance-adjacent scoring product that falls between two jurisdictions, a novel agentic workflow that touches none of the three statutes — fires nothing. No home, no trigger, no record. The detection gate fires on the model before it picks a sector, which is precisely the un-homed emergent zone where foreseeability was already conceded unavailable. The two triggers are not rivals. They are complementary across a coverage boundary, and the boundary is where the hardest harm lives.\n\nThat leaves one unverified assumption holding up the speed claim, and it should be named in the open. The argument that the Equal Credit Opportunity Act or the EEOC can add a court-admissible logging mandate through existing rulemaking — no new legislation — is a legal assertion, not a demonstrated fact. Name the statutory authority. If those agencies already hold the power to compel court-admissible model logging, then credit and employment have a fast home and the un-homed novel class still has none, which only sharpens my point. If fresh authorization is needed, the 'near-term, no new science' advantage collapses and both triggers stand on equal feasibility footing — at which point coverage is the only thing separating them. The harm we cannot route to a regulator is the harm no one will be standing under when it lands.",
