    {"label": "Distinguishing System-Level Signal Extractability From Sector Diligence", "description": "A Belief within safetyist discourse that whether a model failure was detectable rests on a system-level property — model weights combined with the published interpretability tooling frontier at a given time — that is invariant across deployment sectors, separable from the legally constructed standard of care a field applies.\nEncompasses: the distinction between upstream signal existence and downstream sector-specific interpretation; the claim that identical model internals yield identical extractability regardless of deployment context; the framing of detection capacity as an engineering property rather than a legal construct.\nExcludes: foreseeability as a sector-set legal threshold; claims that detectability tracks institutional inspection capacity; doctrines of reasonable diligence calibrated per domain.", "pov": "safetyist", "category": "Beliefs", "rationale": "The central definitional disagreement of the debate — whether reducibility-by-inspection is system-independent or sector-relative like foreseeability — has no existing node capturing the specific claim that signal extractability is a weights-plus-tooling property distinct from sector calibration.", "source_claims": ["C21", "C22"]},
